values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i047$il$, $il$Companies need a real group structure, not just a note saying they are related$il$, $il$Data & schema$il$, $il$Both$il$, $il$must$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-09T12:37:02.000Z'::timestamptz, 1, array[$il$i004$il$,$il$i054$il$,$il$i057$il$,$il$i068$il$]::text[], '{}'::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${}$il$::jsonb,
$il$Today a group relationship is a free text box called Parent Group, and it only points one way. A company can say who its parent is. A parent cannot say who its subsidiaries are. Oliver wants to link from either end.

THE FIX IS NOT A SECOND FIELD. Do not add a Subsidiaries box next to Parent Group. That is two copies of one fact and they will disagree the first time somebody edits one and not the other. Store the relationship ONCE, let it be created or removed from either side, and render it from both: the parent lists its subsidiaries, the subsidiary names its parent.

SHOW IT AT THE TOP, NOT BURIED IN GEOGRAPHY AND STRUCTURE. The group is the first thing that changes how you treat a company, so it belongs in the header next to the priority and research chips, where Duttenhofer currently shows only a dash, Untouched and Germany. Something like Part of Save Group, or 3 companies in this group, as a chip you can click through. Anyone opening a record should see the group before they see anything they might act on.

AND IT HAS TO BE A LINK, NOT TYPED TEXT. Parent Group currently holds a name someone typed. A typed name cannot be clicked, cannot be traversed by a guard, and drifts in spelling. It has to reference an actual company record.

OLIVER ASKED WHETHER THIS IS A MUST. It is, and not for tidiness. i004, the group level duplicate approach guard, is already agreed as a must, and a guard needs something to traverse. Without a real link there is nothing to walk, so i004 cannot be built. That makes this the prerequisite, and it is why it sits in wave 1 where getting it wrong means a migration rather than an edit.

It is also not theoretical. Four group collisions surfaced in five days, and more since: Save Group and asgoodasnew, where a live Monday deal sits on one side and a Pending Review InMail on the other; three unlinked spusu records across Austria, Germany and Italy; four Recommerce records; Telia and its Nordic subsidiaries; Coolblue with a German country manager and no German entity.

The shape should be settled with Brad first. The questions are in the background, and the second one matters more than the schema.$il$,
$il$QUESTION ZERO, NOW THAT THE DIRECTION PROBLEM IS CLEAR: one relationship row between two company records, not a field on either of them. That is what makes it editable from both ends without two sources of truth, and what lets a company sit in a group of more than two. A field on the child cannot express a parent with six subsidiaries without six separate reads.

QUESTION ONE, THE SHAPE. A self referencing parent field on companies is the cheap option and handles a straight tree. A separate groups table is more work and handles the cases where there is no clean parent, for instance a cooperative whose members own it rather than the other way round. Recommendation: a link with a TYPE on it, rather than a bare parent pointer, for the reason in question two.

QUESTION TWO, AND THIS IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS. A corporate tree is not what we actually need. What outreach needs to know is one thing: WOULD APPROACHING THIS COMPANY BE NOTICED BY THAT ONE. Those are different questions, and the real cases already prove it.
Conrad, getgoods and Voelkner is ownership. One approach, one conversation.
EURONICS and Berlet is a cooperative. Berlet is commercially independent and would not blink at a EURONICS conversation, even though they share a brand.
Otto Group is a holding of trading companies that run their own commercial decisions.
spusu Austria, Germany and Italy is one brand across three markets, probably one buying decision.
Flatten all four into parent and you lose exactly the distinction the guard needs. So the link carries a type, and the guard behaves differently per type: block for ownership and one buying centre, warn for holding company, ignore for cooperative membership.

QUESTION THREE, VERIFIED OR NOT. The Save and asgoodasnew link is recorded twice in the lake as probable but unverified, and it has stayed that way for months. If the model has no confidence flag, that guess becomes a fact the moment someone types it into a parent field, and then a guard starts blocking real outreach on the strength of a hunch. Every link needs verified or unverified and a source, and an unverified link should warn rather than block.

QUESTION FOUR, WHO SIGNS. If the group is one buying centre, the pitch goes to the parent and the subsidiaries are volume, not prospects. If they buy independently, each is its own prospect. This is a commercial question, not a data one, and it is the reason to have the conversation with Brad and probably with Mark rather than just picking a schema.

QUESTION FIVE, DO NOT DOUBLE COUNT THE VOLUME. If Save Group and asgoodasnew both carry device numbers and one is the other's channel, summing them at group level invents GWP that does not exist. Group level sizing needs a rule about which entity owns the volume before any roll up is shown, or the board will quietly inflate.

MIGRATION NOTE. The group text already exists as prose on the records that have it. Whoever builds this should harvest those notes rather than start empty, and every harvested link lands as unverified until someone checks it.$il$);

