values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i048$il$, $il$Every lead timeline must be complete, and the record must say how complete it is$il$, $il$Outreach & drafting$il$, $il$Both$il$, $il$must$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-09T12:42:07.000Z'::timestamptz, 1, array[$il$i052$il$,$il$i053$il$,$il$i074$il$]::text[], array[$il$i031$il$]::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${"Brad": "2026-09-18T08:47:43.405Z", "Oliver": "2026-09-15T13:07:52.796Z"}$il$::jsonb,
$il$Every message already sent has to be on the lead's timeline, because the next message is drafted from it. A missing touch does not read as unknown, it reads as never contacted, and that is how someone gets a welcome message they have already had.

ONE CORRECTION UP FRONT, BECAUSE IT CHANGES THE PLAN. Oliver's LinkedIn export will not settle this. LinkedIn's own data export contains DMs only. It has never contained InMails and it never will. So the export fixes the DM backlog and connection state, and leaves every historical InMail exactly as missing as it is today. Those exist only in Oliver's Sales Navigator inbox and no sync, scrape or reconciliation will recover them.

So the plan has three parts, not one. Record at send time, which is the only thing that makes this true going forward. Load the export, which repairs the DM half of the backlog. And mark what is known to be incomplete rather than letting a thin timeline pass as a complete one.

On Phantombuster: nobody has confirmed it can read message history at all, and there is account risk in the answer. Do not plan around it until it is checked. See the background.$il$,
$il$PART ONE, RECORD AT SEND TIME. This is the whole fix going forward and everything else is cleanup. If the message is written and sent through Lovable, the touch is written in the same action, and the timeline cannot drift. It is already a standing rule for the engine and it needs to be a hard constraint of the build rather than a habit. The corollary is uncomfortable but real: anything Oliver sends by hand in Sales Nav, outside the system, will still go missing. Either sends go through Lovable or there is a permanent reconciliation chore.

PART TWO, THE EXPORT. Oliver runs the full LinkedIn data export and hands over the archive. It carries Connections, Invitations sent and received, and Messages, which are DMs. Load them, match on the profile identifier rather than the name, and write what is missing. This is the same archive as i033, so do it once and use it for both the URLs and the history. Depends on i031 having split the URL fields first.

PART THREE, SAY HOW COMPLETE EACH TIMELINE IS. This is the part usually skipped and it is what makes the other two safe. A contact should carry something like history verified to a date, set when the export was loaded or when the first system send happened. A drafter, human or agent, then knows whether a quiet timeline means no contact or means we simply do not know. Without it the backlog repair silently converts unknown into confirmed empty, which is worse than the gap, because now nobody doubts it. Until a contact is verified, the standing caveat still applies: if they are first degree or accepted, treat a prior message as possible and check the thread before sending.

ON PHANTOMBUSTER, honestly. Two things are unknown and both matter. Whether it can read message threads at all, as opposed to profiles and connections, which is what it is mostly used for. And whether reading a Sales Navigator inbox on a schedule puts the account at risk, which is the same caution parked in i019 about automated LinkedIn activity at volume. Losing Oliver's Sales Nav account to recover message history would be a poor trade. Brad should confirm both before it goes in a plan, and if the answer is uncertain, part one plus part three is enough to work from.

WHY IT IS A MUST AND SITS IN WAVE 1. Every guard reads touch history. The cooldown, the do not contact check, the duplicate approach guard, the day seven reply check, the next action derivation in i003 and i036. All of them treat absence as a fact. Feeding them an incomplete timeline does not degrade them gracefully, it makes them confidently wrong, and Catherine Meng's missing 20 August message in i031 is the worked example.$il$);
insert into public.improvement_comments (team_id, improvement_id, who, at, text)
select i.team_id, i.id, $il$Claude$il$, '2026-09-17T12:20:00.000Z'::timestamptz,
$il$17 Sept, a fresh instance and a new problem inside it. Oliver's DM to Marjolein van Osch, Head of eCommerce at Fairphone, 1st degree, sent 7 September at 14:07 from the LinkedIn messaging window, is not in Lovable and not in Sales Navigator. Oliver only found it by opening LinkedIn.

THE NEW PART IS NOT THE GAP, IT IS WHAT THE GAP DISPROVES. This is a plain LinkedIn DM, which is exactly the kind the four hourly watcher is supposed to reconcile, and it has sat unrecorded for ten days. So the fallback that everything else assumes is not working, at least not for threads opened in linkedin.com/messaging rather than in Sales Navigator. Three things to establish, in order: is the watcher running at all, does it read linkedin.com/messaging threads or only Sales Navigator ones, and how far back does it look.

GOOD NEWS ON THIS ONE: a native DM is precisely what the LinkedIn export does contain, so part two of this item would recover it. Oliver has asked for the full export and that is the right call. It still recovers no InMails, ever, and LinkedIn inMail is 315 of the touches on the dashboard, so the export closes the DM half and leaves the InMail half permanently open.$il$
