insert into public.improvements (team_id, item_key, title, category, owner, priority, status, raised_by, created_at, build_wave, blocks, blocked_by, done_by, done_at, pinned, hidden, seen_by, detail, notes)
values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i051$il$, $il$Incoming messages lose their line breaks somewhere between LinkedIn and Lovable$il$, $il$Integrations$il$, $il$Brad$il$, $il$nice$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-09T13:06:26.000Z'::timestamptz, 5, '{}'::text[], array[$il$i037$il$]::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${}$il$::jsonb,
$il$A message arrives from LinkedIn with paragraphs in it and shows up in Lovable as one block. Preserve the original breaks on the way in and on the way out.

THIS IS PROBABLY THE SAME BUG AS i037, AND IT IS THE BETTER CLUE. i037 is that our own drafts come out as one blob, and the open question there was whether the agent is writing them flat or something in the pipe is eating the newlines. An INBOUND message is the control experiment: nobody generated it, LinkedIn definitely sent it with breaks, so if it also arrives flat then the agent is not the problem and the pipe is. Diagnose the two together and one fix may close both.

One thing narrows it further before anyone opens the code. On the Reconciliation screen the messages DO render with their paragraphs intact, in both the unmatched replies Oliver screenshotted. So the breaks survive at least as far as that view. Find out where they are still present and where they are not, and the answer is between those two points.$il$,
$il$THREE PLACES IT CAN BE LOST, and the test above tells you which.
ON INGEST, if the sync strips or collapses whitespace when writing the message. If this were it, Reconciliation would be flat too, and it is not, so probably rule it out.
IN STORAGE, if the field trims. Same reasoning, probably not.
IN A PARTICULAR VIEW, if the container lacks white-space pre-wrap and HTML collapses the newlines on render. This is the most likely one given Reconciliation is fine and other views are not. It is a one line CSS fix per view, which is why it is worth five minutes of finding rather than an hour of guessing.

WHY IT IS WORTH FIXING EVEN THOUGH IT IS COSMETIC. It is not only cosmetic when a person is reading a reply to decide what to say next. A wall of text hides the structure of what someone actually said, and the part that matters, the question or the objection, is usually its own paragraph. Harder to read means more likely to be skimmed, and skimming a prospect's reply is how the next message answers the wrong thing.

IT ALSO FEEDS THE AGENT. The drafting stack reads the thread. If the thread is stored or served flat, the model reads it flat too, and the structure that would have told it which sentence was the ask is gone. So this is not purely a display concern if the loss turns out to be earlier than the view.

CHECK THE ROUND TRIP WHILE YOU ARE THERE, per i037: type a break by hand into a draft field, send it, and confirm it survives to a real sent message. Inbound and outbound share most of the same plumbing.$il$);

insert into public.improvements (team_id, item_key, title, category, owner, priority, status, raised_by, created_at, build_wave, blocks, blocked_by, done_by, done_at, pinned, hidden, seen_by, detail, notes)
values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i052$il$, $il$Reply from inside the conversation, and put every channel in one thread$il$, $il$Outreach & drafting$il$, $il$Both$il$, $il$must$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-15T11:22:20.000Z'::timestamptz, 1, array[$il$i088$il$]::text[], array[$il$i048$il$]::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${"Oliver": "2026-09-15T13:07:47.996Z"}$il$::jsonb,
$il$Urs Moeller at Coolblue replied. On his contact view the Conversation section shows what was sent and his answer, which works well, and there is no way to reply from there. Oliver has to leave, find the thread in Sales Navigator, and answer it somewhere else.

TWO STAGES.
Now: a reply box in the Conversation section, so a reply is written where the thread is read.
Next: every channel in that one thread. LinkedIn DM, InMail and email in a single chronological view, with a channel selector on the reply so Oliver picks how it goes out. In this case they are connected, so it is a free DM.

THIS IS A DATA FIX WEARING A CONVENIENCE HAT, which is why it is a must rather than a nice to have. Replying inside Lovable means the touch is recorded by the same action that sends it. Replying in Sales Navigator means somebody has to remember to log it, and the record of what we have said to people is exactly what keeps going missing. See i048. This feature does not add to that problem, it is one of the few things that actually closes it.$il$,
$il$THE CHANNEL SELECTOR IS NOT A FREE CHOICE, and this is the part to get right or the feature becomes a way to break the rules faster. The channel is already determined by things the system knows:
Connected first degree means a DM, which is free. Not connected means InMail, which costs a credit. That difference is real money and should be shown on the button, not discovered after.
Cold email is permitted for France, Belgium, Netherlands and Switzerland-targeted only. Germany, Austria, Spain, Luxembourg, Italy and Czechia are LinkedIn led. So for a German contact the email option should be visibly unavailable with the reason, not quietly selectable.
Sales Navigator is the default channel even for first degree connections.
So: preselect the correct channel, show why, and make the wrong one refuse rather than warn. A picker that offers five equal options invites the one mistake the guards exist to prevent.

A REPLY TO AN INBOUND IS NOT A COLD TOUCH, so it should not have to go through the full draft review queue. But the hard guards still apply and must run at send: a hard opt out, a do not contact flag, or a company that has moved into Monday all block the send even here. Fail closed, per i005.

EMAIL IS THE LONG POLE and should not hold up stage one. LinkedIn DMs and InMails already arrive through the Sales Navigator integration. Email lives in Outlook and needs its own capture. Two traps when it comes:
Thread on the In-Reply-To and References headers, never on the subject text. Subjects get edited, translated and re-used, and threading on them merges conversations that are not related.
A reply sent from Lovable has to land in Oliver's Outlook Sent Items as well, or his own mailbox stops being a complete record and he ends up checking two places. That is the same split this item is trying to remove.

IT UNLOCKS PROPER DRAFTING, which is the quieter benefit. The standing rule is that no message is drafted without reading the full thread first. Right now the thread is spread across Sales Navigator, Outlook and the touch log, so that rule is followed by hand or not at all. One chronological view is the first time an agent could actually read the conversation before writing into it.

WHAT IS ALREADY GOOD IN THAT VIEW, and should survive the rebuild. The AI state of play panel under Notes is doing real work: it classified Urs's answer as an objection, named it as a differentiation challenge rather than a rejection, placed Coolblue as a sophisticated incumbent on a monthly recurring model, and flagged the open risk carrier question. That is the context a drafter needs, and it is sitting in the right place. The reply box belongs directly under it.

WORTH DECIDING EARLY: does the reply box draft for you, or start blank. Oliver should be able to write two lines himself without waiting for a model. Suggest blank by default with a generate button, rather than an agent filling the box before he has decided what he wants to say.

This is the conversation half of the single GTM system spec. The deal layer went to Brad on 15 September; this is the same argument applied to messages.$il$);
insert into public.improvement_comments (team_id, improvement_id, who, at, text)
select i.team_id, i.id, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-17T14:20:00.000Z'::timestamptz,
$il$17 Sept, Oliver restated this from the Hector Destailleur thread at Recommerce Group and added three things the item did not yet say. Recording them here rather than as a new item, because it is the same feature.

1. HALF OF STAGE TWO IS ALREADY BUILT, which shrinks the job. The Hector thread shows the whole history in one chronological view already: connection request 13 May, InMail 14 May, DM 15 May, his DM reply the same day, the email of 18 May with its subject line, and his DM reply to that. Four channels, both directions, correctly ordered. So the unified view exists. What is missing is only the reply box and the send path underneath it.

2. REPLY IN KIND BY DEFAULT. The channel should preselect to the one THEY last used, not just the cheapest permitted one. Replying where someone wrote to you keeps the thread in one place on their side too. That sits on top of the rules already in the background here, so the precedence is: hard blocks remove options entirely, then match their last inbound channel, then fall back to the cheapest permitted one and say why the default moved.

3. CHANGING THE CHANNEL IS A DELIBERATE ACT AND STARTS SOMETHING NEW. Oliver's words: it should be able to be changed to initiate a new sequence. So switching the picker is not a formatting choice, it opens a new thread on the new channel, and the interface should say so. It matters for threading, for the sequence counter, and for whether the next chaser follows the old conversation or the new one.

AND ONE THING THIS THREAD SHOWS BY ACCIDENT. Hector handed over his address inside a DM, hector.destailleur at recommerce.com, and the email that followed used it. Addresses arrive inside messages and do not reach the Email field, which is empty on most contacts. When an inbound message contains an address, offer to save it to the contact in one click. That is what makes the email option on this picker available at all, and it is cheap next to the enrichment in i015.$il$
from public.improvements i where i.team_id = (select id from public.teams limit 1) and i.item_key = $il$i052$il$;

